## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement policy-based gating and cross-account state references in production workflows.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform Cloud and Sentinel policy gating
- remote_state across AWS accounts
- per-environment workspaces (qa/prod)
- CI integration for plan upload and gate checks

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal Sentinel-like policy (illustrative)
import "tfplan/v2"
import "strings"

let prodChange = false
for (rc in tfplan.resource_changes) {
  if (strings.contains(rc.address, ".prod.")) {
    if (rc.change.actions.includes("create") || rc.change.actions.includes("update") || rc.change.actions.includes("delete")) {
      prodChange = true
    }
  }
}
function main() { return !prodChange }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to handle multi-resource prod groups with blue/green deployments?
- What failure modes exist if remote_state is unavailable during plan?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world Terraform governance requires strict isolation and policy enforcement when multiple teams/environments share global resources. This question probes your ability to combine per-environment workspaces with policy guardrails to prevent cross-environment impact.

## Key Concepts
- Per-environment Terraform Cloud workspaces for isolation
- Provider aliases to target the correct AWS account
- Sentinel policy to guard changes to shared VPC components
- CI validation: parse plan JSON and enforce env-tag constraints

## Code Example
```javascript
// Sentinel-like illustrative policy (Terraform Cloud Sentinel syntax would differ)
policy "env_guard" {
  rule deny_shared_vpc_changes {
    // deny any change to shared VPC unless resource.tags.env == workspace.name
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle tag drift across environments?
- How would you test policy changes locally before pushing to CI?
